---
/*
 * The Open Source Initiative keyhole, in the landing page's lead.
 *
 * The mark, its link, and the conditions it is drawn under live in one file
 * because they are one thing. OSI publishes a trademark licence rather than a
 * permission form: written permission is not required for a business website
 * provided five conditions hold, and four of them are satisfied somewhere
 * other than in this file.
 *
 *   Promote OSI-approved licences  the AGPL-3.0 cell in the feature band
 *   No disparagement               nothing on this site is about OSI
 *   No implied endorsement         one glyph at text size, never at badge
 *                                  scale — see `.osi-mark` in global.css
 *   ® and an attribution statement the footer, on every page, verbatim
 *   Follow the logo guidelines     the artwork and palette below
 *
 * `tests/site-content.test.mjs` asserts the ones that live elsewhere, keyed on
 * the mark being present, so removing a footer paragraph or rewriting the
 * feature band fails the build for the page carrying the mark.
 *
 * THE ARTWORK, which is the whole reason this file was rewritten. The path and
 * the palette moved to `src/lib/osi-artwork.mjs` when the closing band's star
 * field became a second consumer; the reasoning stayed here.
 *
 * The first version drew simple-icons' `opensourceinitiative` path. That is a
 * faithful copy of OSI's black-and-white file and it was still the wrong
 * choice, because OSI's B&W variant is the *outline* form: a ring whose wall
 * is 0.8 units on a 24 viewBox. At this paragraph's 19.43px that wall renders
 * 0.65px wide. It inked 14.4% of its own box next to a bolt at 25.2% and a
 * battery at 94.5%, and it read as a grey smudge rather than as a logo. The
 * repair that would fix it — a thicker stroke — is the one thing the
 * guidelines forbid outright: never alter or add elements to the logo.
 *
 * OSI's standard logo is not an outline. It is a solid body with the keyhole
 * knocked out of it, and so is the greyscale variant. The outline file is for
 * reproduction at sizes where a hairline survives; this is not one. So the
 * path here is the solid mark, taken from the public-domain SVG of the full
 * logo on Wikimedia Commons — its `#shape` element, which is the mark alone,
 * cropped to a tight viewBox. It inks 66.8%, which is inside the range the two
 * emoji beside it occupy, and it is legible at 19px because it was drawn to be.
 *
 * Colour is not a choice here. Every other mark on this site is `--oe-muted`
 * and `platform-marks.mjs` argues at length for dropping brand colour; this one
 * inverts that rule because its licence inverts it — "never stray from the
 * color palette", and the palette is #3DA639, #1E531D, #000000. `--oe-muted`
 * would be the violation. The source file ships #3da638 over #1c511c, a hair
 * off OSI's published values; the published values are used instead.
 *
 * One artwork in both themes. #3DA639 is 6.19:1 on the dark canvas and 2.93:1
 * on the light one — under the 3:1 this site holds graphics to, taken as the
 * logotype exemption in WCAG 1.4.11, because the sentence beside it says
 * "Fully open source" in words and the mark carries no information a reader
 * has to extract from it. The dark-green outline is a 0.64px edge at this size
 * rather than anything load-bearing.
 *
 * No ® on the glyph. In OSI's own artwork the ® attaches to the logotype, not
 * to the keyhole — in the source SVG it is a separate `registered_tm` path
 * inside the text group — and the logotype is not displayed here. The
 * acknowledgement rides on the attribution statement in the footer instead,
 * which is where a 1.7px symbol would have been unreadable anyway.
 *
 * THE SECOND INSTANCE, and the one place this file's reasoning does not hold.
 * The closing band draws this mark again, at badge scale, as a field of lights,
 * and in the page's own ink. That breaks three of the conditions set out above:
 * "never alter or add elements to the logo", the text-size treatment this file
 * relies on to keep "no implied endorsement" true, and "never stray from the
 * color palette" — the paragraph above calls `--oe-muted` the violation, and
 * the field uses it. Drawn at the founder's direction after each condition was
 * put to him in writing; the last of them was directed after the field had
 * first been built keeping OSI's green, because one mark in its own colour
 * beside two in the page's read as a mistake rather than as a licence being
 * honoured. What survives there is the link: the one below is still the site's
 * hyperlink to opensource.org, and the field is decorative and carries no
 * accessible name of its own. See `StarfieldMark.astro`.
 *
 * The link is not optional and it is not free: "The OSI Logo must be
 * hyperlinked to opensource.org where technically feasible." It is feasible,
 * so this is an outbound link sitting above the fold beside the one primary
 * action, which is a cost the page pays because the condition is not ours to
 * waive.
 */
import { OSI_BODY, OSI_EDGE, OSI_EDGE_WIDTH, OSI_PATH, OSI_VIEW_BOX } from '../lib/osi-artwork.mjs';
---

{/* aria-hidden on the drawing because the visible words beside it already name
    it, and the link needs an accessible name of its own. The build rewrite in
    `scripts/external-links.mjs` appends "(opens in a new tab)" to this label —
    text appended inside an icon-only link is never announced. */}
<a class="osi-mark" href="https://opensource.org" aria-label="Open Source Initiative">
  <svg viewBox={OSI_VIEW_BOX} aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
    <path
      d={OSI_PATH}
      fill={OSI_BODY}
      stroke={OSI_EDGE}
      stroke-width={OSI_EDGE_WIDTH}
      stroke-linecap="round"
      stroke-linejoin="round"></path>
  </svg>
</a>
